-- Complete example for the "Multi-Temporal Rule Modeling" tutorial.
--
-- Scenario: a GST/VAT-style tax rate rose from 7% to 9%, effective on
-- rules dated 2024-01-01 or later. We model the rate as a function of
-- "which version of the law applies" (RULES EFFECTIVE DATE), and explore
-- how that axis interacts with the other two time axes L4 tracks:
-- system time (the evaluation clock) and valid time (when the facts held).

IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

TIMEZONE IS "Etc/UTC"

§ `The rule`

-- GST rose from 7% to 9% for rules effective on/after 2024-01-01.
`GST rate` MEANS
  IF (DATE_SERIAL `RULES EFFECTIVE DATE`) AT LEAST (DATE_SERIAL (Date 1 1 2024))
  THEN 9
  ELSE 7

GIVEN amount IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`GST payable on` amount MEANS amount TIMES `GST rate` DIVIDED BY 100

§ `Step 1 -- ambient: no axis pinned, so law-time falls back to today`

-- Whatever "today" is when you run this file, it's well after the
-- 2024-01-01 cutover, so the ambient rate is the new one.
#EVAL `GST rate`

§ `Step 2 -- pin the rule-version explicitly`

-- expected: 7 -- old law, regardless of when this is run or what the
-- facts say
#EVAL `EVAL UNDER RULES EFFECTIVE AT` (Date 1 6 2023) `GST rate`

-- expected: 9 -- new law
#EVAL `EVAL UNDER RULES EFFECTIVE AT` (Date 1 7 2024) `GST rate`

-- expected: 70 -- composes through a dependent rule, old version
#EVAL `EVAL UNDER RULES EFFECTIVE AT` (Date 1 6 2023) (`GST payable on` 1000)

-- expected: 90 -- dependent rule, new version
#EVAL `EVAL UNDER RULES EFFECTIVE AT` (Date 1 7 2024) (`GST payable on` 1000)

§ `Step 3 -- the default: law-time tracks fact-time`

-- No rule-version pinned here -- only the *facts* are pinned, to 2019.
-- RULES EFFECTIVE DATE falls back to the valid-time axis, so the OLD
-- law applies: this models the presumption against retroactivity
-- (apply the law that was in force when the facts occurred).
--
-- expected: 7
#EVAL `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 1 6 2019) `GST rate`

-- expected: 9 -- facts pinned to 2024 instead: new regime
#EVAL `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 1 7 2024) `GST rate`

§ `Step 4 -- an explicit rule-version pin overrides fact-time`

-- Facts are in 2024 (new regime by default), but we explicitly ask for
-- the 2019 law anyway. The explicit, inner override wins: this is how
-- you deliberately decouple "when the facts happened" from "which law
-- to apply" -- e.g. a savings clause, a transitional provision, or an
-- audit of what an old ruling would look like restated in code.
--
-- expected: 7
#EVAL `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 1 7 2024) (`EVAL UNDER RULES EFFECTIVE AT` (Date 1 6 2019) `GST rate`)

§ `Step 5 -- TODAY is independent of valid time and rule-effective time`

-- TODAY only ever reads the SYSTEM TIME axis. Overriding valid time (or
-- the rule-version) must never perturb it -- otherwise a limitations
-- countdown or "days since filing" calculation would silently shift
-- whenever you evaluated a rule about a past fact.
--
-- Same result with or without a valid-time override: TODAY doesn't care.
#EVAL `EVAL UNDER VALID TIME` (Date 1 6 2019) (DATE_SERIAL TODAY)
#EVAL DATE_SERIAL TODAY

§ `Step 6 -- auditing a past evaluation clock with system time`

-- EVAL AS OF SYSTEM TIME overrides yet another axis: the evaluation
-- clock itself. It answers "what would the system have computed if
-- this had been run back then?" -- useful for audit trails and
-- regression snapshots.
--
-- Careful: RULES EFFECTIVE DATE's own fallback chain bottoms out at
-- "today" (same computation as the TODAY builtin) when NEITHER the
-- rule-version nor the valid-time axis is pinned. Since TODAY reads
-- system time, overriding system time alone flows through to that
-- fallback -- it's only inert when an explicit rule-version or
-- valid-time pin is already present to take priority over it.
--
-- expected: 7 -- no rule-version or valid-time pinned, so the fallback
-- resolves via the (overridden) system clock: 2020-06-01 is pre-cutover
#EVAL `EVAL AS OF SYSTEM TIME` (Date 1 6 2020) `GST rate`

§ `Step 7 -- scanning a range of dates`

-- VALUE AT stamps BOTH valid-time and rule-effective-time to the given
-- day, then evaluates the expression -- handy for building a
-- day-indexed table of what a rule would say across a changeover.
--
-- expected: 7 -- VALUE AT's per-day stamp is load-bearing
#EVAL `VALUE AT` (Date 1 6 2023) (GIVEN d YIELD `GST rate`)

-- expected: 9
#EVAL `VALUE AT` (Date 1 7 2024) (GIVEN d YIELD `GST rate`)

-- EVER/ALWAYS BETWEEN and WHEN LAST/NEXT scan a date range for a
-- predicate of DATE, independently of the rule-version machinery above --
-- they're general date-search builtins, shown here on `is weekend`.
`weekend?` d MEANS `is weekend` (DATE_SERIAL d)

-- expected: TRUE -- Jan 6-7 2024 includes a Saturday/Sunday
#EVAL `EVER BETWEEN` (Date 5 1 2024) (Date 7 1 2024) `weekend?`

-- expected: TRUE -- Jan 6-7 2024 is a Sat/Sun weekend throughout
#EVAL `ALWAYS BETWEEN` (Date 6 1 2024) (Date 7 1 2024) `weekend?`

-- expected: the most recent weekend day on/before 2024-01-10
#EVAL `WHEN LAST` (Date 10 1 2024) `weekend?`

-- expected: the next weekend day on/after 2024-01-10
#EVAL `WHEN NEXT` (Date 10 1 2024) `weekend?`
